.TH DFTE 1 2026-10-30 "dfte 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
dfte \- send files directly to a nearby device, without a server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B id
.br
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B receive
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-once ]
.RB [ \-\-accept\-from
.IR fingerprint ]
.RI [ more\ options ]
.br
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B send
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-\-expect
.IR fingerprint ]
.RI [ more\ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B peers
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR seconds ]
.br
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B trust
.BR list | add | remove
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B resumable
.RB [ \-\-discard
.IR id ]
.br
.B dfte
.RI [ options ]
.B qr
.RB [ \-\-ssid
.IR ssid ]
.RB [ \-\-psk
.IR psk ]
.RB [ \-\-ip
.IR ip ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dfte
copies files and folders from one device to another over a local network or a
Wi\-Fi Direct link.
No server, account or Internet connection is involved.
The other device can run
.BR dfte ,
.BR dfte\-gtk (1)
or the DFTE Android app.
.PP
Connections are encrypted and authenticated.
The first time two devices connect, both show the other's fingerprint and a
six\-digit security code.
Accept only if both screens show the same code.
A device you mark as trusted, or whose fingerprint you pass with
.BR \-\-to ,
.B \-\-expect
or
.BR \-\-accept\-from ,
skips that prompt.
There is no option to skip it otherwise.
When standard input is not a terminal, the prompt cannot be shown and the
peer is rejected unless it is trusted or pinned.
.PP
Devices on the same network find each other with signed UDP beacons on port
47820 (multicast group 239.255.70.84, plus any
.B \-\-beacon\-to
addresses).
Received data is written to a staging directory first and only moved to the
destination once its BLAKE3 hash matches the sender's.
An interrupted transfer resumes when the sender sends the same files again.
.PP
Progress and prompts go to standard error.
Results go to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
These work with every command.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More log output on standard error: once for info, twice for debug, three times
for trace.
Logs never contain keys or file contents.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output on standard output.
Transfer reports are printed as one JSON object per line.
Errors become a JSON object with
.BR error ,
.B exit_code
and
.B message
fields.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS id
Show this device's name and fingerprint, and where the key file is.
The identity is created the first time any command needs it.
.SS receive
Wait for files from another device.
By default
.B dfte receive
keeps waiting for further transfers after each one; stop it with Ctrl\-C.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " dir"
Where finished files go.
Default: your Downloads folder (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
Port to listen on, 0 for any free port.
Default: 47821.
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " address"
Address to listen on.
Default: 0.0.0.0.
.TP
.BI \-\-transport " auto|tcp|quic"
Backends to accept.
.B auto
(the default) listens on TCP and QUIC on the same port, since each sender
picks its own.
.TP
.B \-\-once
Exit after one transfer attempt instead of waiting for more.
.TP
.BI \-\-accept\-from " fingerprint"
Accept only this device, without the code prompt.
Get its fingerprint from its own screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-beacon\-bind " address:port"
Local address for discovery beacons.
Default: 0.0.0.0:47820.
.TP
.BI \-\-beacon\-to " ip:port"
Also exchange beacons with this address on the local network.
Repeatable.
Use it on networks that drop multicast.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-multicast
Don't use multicast discovery; only the
.B \-\-beacon\-to
targets.
.TP
.BI \-\-wifi\-direct " device"
First join a Wi\-Fi Direct group with this device, given by its Wi\-Fi Direct
name or P2P address.
Linux only, through NetworkManager; no root needed.
See
.BR NOTES .
.SS send
Send files or folders to another device.
Folders are sent with their contents; symbolic links inside them are skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " device"
The receiving device: a fingerprint, the name of a trusted device, the name
it advertises, or
.IR ip : port .
Without it,
.B dfte
looks for devices and asks which one to use, which needs a terminal.
A fingerprint or trusted name also pins the peer, so no code prompt is shown.
.TP
.BI \-\-expect " fingerprint"
With
.BI \-\-to " ip:port"\c
: the receiver's fingerprint, read from its screen or QR code.
Skips the code prompt; a different key is rejected.
.TP
.BI \-\-streams " n"
Parallel data streams, 1 to 16.
Default: 2.
.TP
.BI \-\-transport " auto|tcp|quic"
.B auto
(the default) picks this device's best backend: TCP if the kernel has no UDP
segmentation offload (Linux before 4.18), QUIC otherwise.
The receiver must accept the chosen backend; its
.B auto
takes both.
.TP
.BI \-\-scan\-timeout " seconds"
How long to look for devices.
Default: 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-beacon\-bind " address:port"
As for
.BR receive .
.TP
.BI \-\-beacon\-to " ip:port"
As for
.BR receive .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-multicast
As for
.BR receive .
.TP
.BI \-\-wifi\-direct " device"
As for
.BR receive .
When the other device ends up owning the group and
.B \-\-to
is not an address, the files go to the group owner on port 47821.
.SS peers
List devices nearby that are ready to receive, with their fingerprints and
addresses.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " seconds"
How long to listen.
Default: 5.
.PP
.BR \-\-beacon\-bind ,
.BR \-\-beacon\-to ,
.B \-\-no\-multicast
and
.B \-\-wifi\-direct
work as for
.BR receive .
.SS trust
Manage trusted devices.
A trusted device skips the code comparison as long as its key stays the same.
.TP
.B trust list
List trusted devices.
.TP
.BI "trust add " "fingerprint name"
Trust a device whose fingerprint you compared on its own screen.
.TP
.BI "trust remove " who
Stop trusting a device, given by fingerprint or name.
The code is asked again next time.
.PP
Answering
.B a
at the code prompt also adds the device.
.SS resumable
List unfinished incoming transfers: their id, how much has arrived, and the
first file names.
They continue on their own when the sender sends the same files again.
Matching is by path, size and content hash, so the sender may use a new
command line.
.TP
.BI \-\-discard " id"
Delete the partial data of this transfer.
.SS qr
Print the QR payload an iPhone would scan to join this device's hotspot.
This is a preview:
.B dfte
does not start a hotspot (that needs root, and is not implemented), and there
is no iPhone app yet.
The payload contains the passphrase, so only show it to the person you are
pairing with.
.TP
.BI \-\-ssid " ssid"
Hotspot name.
Generated if omitted.
.TP
.BI \-\-psk " psk"
Hotspot passphrase, 8 to 63 characters.
Generated if omitted.
.TP
.BI \-\-ip " ip"
This device's address on its hotspot.
Default: 192.168.50.1.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
Engine port the iPhone connects to.
Default: 47821.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 5
.B 0
Success.
.TP 5
.B 1
Unexpected error.
.TP 5
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP 5
.B 3
Connection lost.
.TP 5
.B 4
Security check failed.
.TP 5
.B 5
Declined by the other device.
.TP 5
.B 6
Not enough space.
.TP 5
.B 7
Source file problem.
.TP 5
.B 8
Integrity check failed.
.TP 5
.B 9
Incompatible app version.
.TP 5
.B 10
The other device broke the protocol.
.TP 5
.B 11
Identity or configuration problem.
.TP 5
.B 12
Device not found.
.TP 5
.B 130
Interrupted; resume state was kept.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DFTE_DEVICE_NAME
Name shown to other devices.
Default: the host name.
The name is not authenticated; other devices should rely on the fingerprint.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Base for the state directory, see
.BR FILES .
Default:
.IR ~/.local/share .
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Base for the sender's hash cache.
Default:
.IR ~/.cache .
.TP
.B XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR
Default destination for received files.
If unset,
.B dfte
reads it from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/user\-dirs.dirs ,
and falls back to
.IR ~/Downloads .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where
.I user\-dirs.dirs
is looked up.
Default:
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B HOME
Used for the defaults above.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter in
.B tracing\-subscriber
syntax, for example
.BR debug " or " dfte_core=trace .
Overrides
.BR \-v .
.PP
Relative or empty values of the
.B XDG_*
variables are ignored, as the XDG Base Directory specification requires.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/dfte/
State directory, mode 0700.
Shared with
.BR dfte\-gtk (1),
so both are the same device.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/dfte/identity.key
This device's Ed25519 secret key, mode 0600.
.B dfte
refuses to use it if other users can read it.
Deleting it gives the device a new identity, and every other device will ask
for a code comparison again.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/dfte/trusted.json
Trusted devices, mode 0600.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/dfte/staging/
Partial downloads
.RI ( *.part )
and their resume state
.RI ( *.resume.json ).
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/dfte/hashes/
Sender's cache of file hashes, so unchanged files are not read twice.
Safe to delete.
.SH EXAMPLES
Receive into the Downloads folder and wait for transfers:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dfte receive
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send a file and a folder, choosing the receiver from a menu:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dfte send report.pdf photos/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send to a known address, checking the receiver's fingerprint instead of
comparing codes:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dfte send \-\-to 192.168.1.20:47821 \e
    \-\-expect 8bee\-594a\-310a\-934b\-0f81\-3b14 big.iso
.EE
.RE
.PP
Receive one transfer from a pinned device, for a script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dfte receive \-\-once \-\-accept\-from 8bee\-594a\-310a\-934b\-0f81\-3b14 \e
    \-\-dir /srv/incoming \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send to a phone over Wi\-Fi Direct, with DFTE open on the phone's Send or
Receive screen:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dfte send \-\-wifi\-direct "Galaxy A30" holiday.mp4
.EE
.RE
.PP
On a network that drops multicast, point both sides at each other:
.PP
.RS
.EX
dfte receive \-\-beacon\-to 192.168.1.30:47820
dfte send \-\-beacon\-to 192.168.1.20:47820 notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B \-\-wifi\-direct
uses NetworkManager's Wi\-Fi P2P support, which only starts connections.
The other device must be waiting for one: on Android, DFTE's Send or Receive
screen.
Two machines that both rely on NetworkManager are not expected to reach each
other this way (untested).
If negotiation makes this machine the group owner, NetworkManager needs
.B dnsmasq
installed to give the other device an address.
A Wi\-Fi Direct client interface left behind by a crash can be cleared with
.BR "nmcli radio wifi off && nmcli radio wifi on" .
.PP
The identity key is stored as a plain file, not in the desktop keyring.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dfte\-gtk (1),
.BR nmcli (1)
.PP
The project's
.IR docs/security.md ,
.I docs/wifi\-direct.md
and
.IR docs/protocol.md .
